WEBVTT

00:00.490 --> 00:03.565
A parcel company promises four deliveries by noon.

00:03.565 --> 00:05.951
At one in the afternoon, two have arrived on

00:05.951 --> 00:09.253
time, one arrived late, and one has not arrived.

00:09.253 --> 00:11.012
This is an invented example.

00:11.012 --> 00:13.162
Assume the records are complete at that moment.

00:14.377 --> 00:18.926
First, ask: of the completed deliveries, how many were on time?

00:18.926 --> 00:20.912
Three parcels have arrived.

00:20.912 --> 00:22.602
Two were on time.

00:22.602 --> 00:25.888
Two out of three gives 66.7%.

00:25.888 --> 00:28.532
The unfinished delivery is outside this calculation.

00:29.587 --> 00:33.002
Now ask: of all deliveries due, how many arrived on time?

00:33.002 --> 00:35.939
All four parcels were promised by noon, so all

00:35.939 --> 00:38.134
four count, including the unfinished delivery.

00:38.134 --> 00:39.903
Two out of four gives 50.0%.

00:40.885 --> 00:44.021
Both calculations are valid for their stated questions.

00:44.021 --> 00:48.316
The difference is the denominator: the total number of deliveries included.

00:48.316 --> 00:51.828
Three completed deliveries, or all four deliveries due.

00:51.828 --> 00:55.295
A dashboard labelled only on-time delivery leaves readers to guess.

00:56.254 --> 01:02.675
Metadata is information that describes data: its structure, meaning, origin and use.

01:02.675 --> 01:05.194
Column names and types are metadata.

01:05.194 --> 01:09.388
So are a metric’s definition and a record of the job that produced it.

01:09.388 --> 01:11.930
The values alone rarely explain what they measure.

01:12.750 --> 01:14.965
Suppose Delivery Operations wants to know

01:14.965 --> 01:17.878
whether all promised deliveries arrived on time.

01:17.878 --> 01:22.484
Record the decision: on time means delivered at or before the promised time.

01:22.484 --> 01:26.272
Include every delivery due by noon, even if it has not arrived.

01:26.272 --> 01:28.814
Measure at one in the afternoon, and name Delivery

01:28.814 --> 01:31.433
Operations as the team responsible for the definition.

01:32.417 --> 01:34.814
This definition makes the choice inspectable.

01:34.814 --> 01:39.160
All four parcels belong in the denominator, so the measure gives 50.0%.

01:39.160 --> 01:42.243
Another team can legitimately use completed deliveries,

01:42.243 --> 01:44.629
provided its definition makes that choice clear.

01:45.904 --> 01:48.512
Field names need explanation too.

01:48.512 --> 01:51.581
A column named delivered_at could record when a parcel

01:51.581 --> 01:54.757
reached a depot, or when it reached the customer.

01:54.757 --> 01:56.829
Both are plausible meanings.

01:56.829 --> 01:59.496
The name alone cannot tell us which one applies.

02:00.620 --> 02:02.668
But a definition is not proof.

02:02.668 --> 02:08.958
If the dashboard removes unfinished deliveries before counting, it shows 66.7%, even

02:08.958 --> 02:12.105
though Delivery Operations’ definition requires 50.0%.

02:12.105 --> 02:15.639
The query can run successfully and still answer the wrong question.

02:16.583 --> 02:19.485
The recorded rule gives a reviewer something to check.

02:19.485 --> 02:20.863
Inspect the filter.

02:20.863 --> 02:22.656
Inspect the denominator.

02:22.656 --> 02:25.583
Compare the calculation with the four-parcel example.

02:25.583 --> 02:29.485
If the intended rule changes, review both the definition and the calculation.

02:30.760 --> 02:33.763
When you see a number, ask what counts, when it was

02:33.763 --> 02:36.685
measured, and which team can explain its meaning.

02:36.685 --> 02:40.140
Then check that the calculation follows the recorded rule.

02:40.140 --> 02:42.289
Metadata makes those questions answerable.
